    glossary: {
        eyebrow: 'Words explained',
        title: 'Glossary',
        intro: 'Some words on this website are unusual or have a special meaning in our work. Here we explain them in plain language.',
        toc: 'Terms A–Z',
        items: [
            {
                id: 'decolonial',
                term: 'Decolonial',
                text: 'Colonialism did not only take land and resources – it also shaped how history is told and whose voices count. A decolonial approach questions these ways of thinking and makes room for perspectives that were silenced. “Decolonization” originally means the political process in which colonies became independent.'
            },
            {
                id: 'dekolonizart',
                term: 'DekolonizArt',
                text: 'Our name combines the ideas of decolonization and artistic creation. We believe that art can question inherited narratives, recover overlooked histories and imagine new possibilities for the future.'
            },
            {
                id: 'african-perspectives',
                term: 'African / Africanist perspectives',
                text: 'Looking at history from the point of view of African people and societies – instead of only from the point of view of the colonial powers, which has long dominated history books.'
            },
            {
                id: 'remembrance',
                term: 'Remembrance',
                text: 'Actively keeping the memory of past events alive. For us, remembrance is a creative act that restores forgotten voices and opens new possibilities – “the Art of Remembrance, the Power of Renewal”.'
            },
            {
                id: 'ubuntu',
                term: 'Ubuntu',
                text: 'A philosophy from southern Africa, often translated as “I am because we are”. It means that a person becomes a person through other people – community and humanity belong together.'
            },
            {
                id: 'memo',
                term: 'MeMo',
                text: 'DekolonizArt’s participatory art project. It combines theatre, dance, movement and music and works with school pupils, young people aged 8 to 18, adults and migrant communities on memory, identity and resistance.'
            },
            {
                id: 'participatory',
                term: 'Participatory',
                text: 'Participants do not just watch – they actively shape the work: they bring in their own experiences, develop scenes, dances and songs together and can present them at the end.'
            },
            {
                id: 'duala-manga-bell',
                term: 'Rudolf Duala Manga Bell',
                text: 'King of the Duala people in Cameroon (1873–1914), when Cameroon was a German colony. He resisted the plans of the German colonial administration to expropriate the Duala from their land and was executed in 1914. Our play “Douala Manga Bell – Der König der sein Recht wollte” (The King Who Wanted His Rights) tells his story.'
            },
            {
                id: 'theatrical-reading',
                term: 'Theatrical reading',
                text: 'A play is read aloud by actors, with music, movement and some staging – but before the full theatre production with set and costumes. Our first public reading is in German.'
            },
            {
                id: 'global-village',
                term: 'Berlin Global Village',
                text: 'A centre in Berlin-Neukölln (Am Sudhaus 2, 12053 Berlin) where organisations working on development, migration and global justice come together. Our performances take place in its Miriam Makeba Saal, a hall named after the South African singer and activist Miriam Makeba.'
            },
            {
                id: 'kultur-macht-stark',
                term: 'Kultur macht stark',
                lang: 'de',
                text: 'A funding programme of the German federal government (“Culture makes you strong. Alliances for education”). It supports cultural education projects for children and young people outside school. Our youth project “Theater & Geschichte” is funded by it.'
            }
        ]
    },
